            <ArticleTitle>Usefulness of elevated levels of soluble vascular cell adhesion molecule-1 in predicting in-hospital prognosis in patients with unstable angina pectoris.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1195-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>To assess the in-hospital prognostic value of cellular adhesion molecules (CAMs), the levels of soluble CAMs were measured at admission in 114 patients with severe unstable angina. Patients with an eventful in-hospital course (death, nonfatal acute myocardial infarction, and recurrence of angina) had higher levels of soluble vascular cell adhesion molecule-1 than those without events (p = 0.01); this association was independent of classic risk factors and C-reactive protein.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
